## Detailed Description

QByteArray can be used to store both raw bytes (including '\\\\0's) and traditional 8-bit
'\\\\0'-terminated strings. Using QByteArray is much more convenient than using `const
char *`. Behind the scenes, it always ensures that the data is followed by a '\\\\0'
terminator, and uses [implicit
sharing](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/implicit-sharing.html) (copy-on-write) to
reduce memory usage and avoid needless copying of data.

In addition to QByteArray, Qt also provides the
[QString](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qstring.html) class to store string data.
For most purposes, [QString](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qstring.html) is the
class you want to use. It stores 16-bit Unicode characters, making it easy to store
non-ASCII/non-Latin-1 characters in your application. Furthermore,
[QString](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qstring.html) is used throughout in the Qt
API. The two main cases where QByteArray is appropriate are when you need to store raw
binary data, and when memory conservation is critical (e.g., with Qt for Embedded
Linux).

One way to initialize a QByteArray is simply to pass a `const char *` to its
constructor. For example, the following code creates a byte array of size 5 containing
the data "Hello":

```
QByteArray ba("Hello");
```

Although the [size](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#size)() is 5, the
byte array also maintains an extra '\\\\0' character at the end so that if a function is
used that asks for a pointer to the underlying data (e.g. a call to
[data](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#data)()), the data pointed to
is guaranteed to be '\\\\0'-terminated.

QByteArray makes a deep copy of the `const char *` data, so you can modify it later
without experiencing side effects. (If for performance reasons you don't want to take a
deep copy of the character data, use
[QByteArray::fromRawData](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#fromRawData)()
instead.)

Another approach is to set the size of the array using
[resize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#resize)() and to initialize
the data byte per byte. QByteArray uses 0-based indexes, just like C\\+\\+ arrays. To
access the byte at a particular index position, you can use operator\\[\\](). On non-const
byte arrays, operator\\[\\]() returns a reference to a byte that can be used on the left
side of an assignment. For example:

```
QByteArray ba;
ba.resize(5);
ba[0] = 0x3c;
ba[1] = 0xb8;
ba[2] = 0x64;
ba[3] = 0x18;
ba[4] = 0xca;
```

For read-only access, an alternative syntax is to use
[at](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#at)():

```
for (int i = 0; i < ba.size(); ++i) {
    if (ba.at(i) >= 'a' && ba.at(i) <= 'f')
        cout << "Found character in range [a-f]" << Qt::endl;
}
```

[at](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#at)() can be faster than
operator\\[\\](), because it never causes a [deep
copy](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/implicit-sharing.html#deep-copy) to occur.

To extract many bytes at a time, use
[left](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#left)(),
[right](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#right)(), or
[mid](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#mid)().

A QByteArray can embed '\\\\0' bytes. The
[size](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#size)() function always
returns the size of the whole array, including embedded '\\\\0' bytes, but excluding the
terminating '\\\\0' added by QByteArray. For example:

```
QByteArray ba1("ca\\0r\\0t");
ba1.size();                     // Returns 2.
ba1.constData();                // Returns "ca" with terminating \\0.

QByteArray ba2("ca\\0r\\0t", 3);
ba2.size();                     // Returns 3.
ba2.constData();                // Returns "ca\\0" with terminating \\0.

QByteArray ba3("ca\\0r\\0t", 4);
ba3.size();                     // Returns 4.
ba3.constData();                // Returns "ca\\0r" with terminating \\0.

const char cart[] = {'c', 'a', '\\0', 'r', '\\0', 't'};
QByteArray ba4(QByteArray::fromRawData(cart, 6));
ba4.size();                     // Returns 6.
ba4.constData();                // Returns "ca\\0r\\0t" without terminating \\0.
```

If you want to obtain the length of the data up to and excluding the first '\\\\0'
character, call [qstrlen](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#qstrlen)()
on the byte array.

After a call to [resize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#resize)(),
newly allocated bytes have undefined values. To set all the bytes to a particular value,
call [fill](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#fill)().

To obtain a pointer to the actual character data, call
[data](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#data)() or
[constData](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#constData)(). These
functions return a pointer to the beginning of the data. The pointer is guaranteed to
remain valid until a non-const function is called on the QByteArray. It is also
guaranteed that the data ends with a '\\\\0' byte unless the QByteArray was created from a
[raw data](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#fromRawData). This '\\\\0'
byte is automatically provided by QByteArray and is not counted in
[size](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#size)().

QByteArray provides the following basic functions for modifying the byte data:
[append](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#append)(),
[prepend](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#prepend)(),
[insert](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#insert)(),
[replace](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#replace)(), and
[remove](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#remove)(). For example:

```
QByteArray x("and");
x.prepend("rock ");         // x == "rock and"
x.append(" roll");          // x == "rock and roll"
x.replace(5, 3, "&");       // x == "rock & roll"
```

The [replace](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#replace)() and
[remove](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#remove)() functions' first
two arguments are the position from which to start erasing and the number of bytes that
should be erased.

When you [append](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#append)() data to a
non-empty array, the array will be reallocated and the new data copied to it. You can
avoid this behavior by calling
[reserve](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#reserve)(), which
preallocates a certain amount of memory. You can also call
[capacity](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#capacity)() to find out
how much memory QByteArray actually allocated. Data appended to an empty array is not
copied.

A frequent requirement is to remove whitespace characters from a byte array ('\\\\n',
'\\\\t', ' ', etc.). If you want to remove whitespace from both ends of a QByteArray, use
[trimmed](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#trimmed)(). If you want to
remove whitespace from both ends and replace multiple consecutive whitespaces with a
single space character within the byte array, use
[simplified](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#simplified)().

If you want to find all occurrences of a particular character or substring in a
QByteArray, use [indexOf](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#indexOf)()
or [lastIndexOf](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#lastIndexOf)(). The
former searches forward starting from a given index position, the latter searches
backward. Both return the index position of the character or substring if they find it;
otherwise, they return -1. For example, here's a typical loop that finds all occurrences
of a particular substring:

```
QByteArray ba("We must be <b>bold</b>, very <b>bold</b>");
int j = 0;
while ((j = ba.indexOf("<b>", j)) != -1) {
    cout << "Found <b> tag at index position " << j << Qt::endl;
    ++j;
}
```

If you simply want to check whether a QByteArray contains a particular character or
substring, use
[contains](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#contains)(). If you want
to find out how many times a particular character or substring occurs in the byte array,
use [count](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#count-3)(). If you want
to replace all occurrences of a particular value with another, use one of the
two-parameter [replace](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#replace)()
overloads.

[QByteArray](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html)s can be compared using
overloaded operators such as operator\\<(), operator\\<\\=(), operator\\=\\=(),
operator\\>\\=(), and so on. The comparison is based exclusively on the numeric values of
the characters and is very fast, but is not what a human would expect.
[QString::localeAwareCompare](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qstring.html#localeAwareCompare)()
is a better choice for sorting user-interface strings.

For historical reasons, QByteArray distinguishes between a null byte array and an empty
byte array. A *null* byte array is a byte array that is initialized using QByteArray's
default constructor or by passing (const char \\*)0 to the constructor. An *empty* byte
array is any byte array with size 0\\. A null byte array is always empty, but an empty
byte array isn't necessarily null:

```
QByteArray().isNull();          // returns true
QByteArray().isEmpty();         // returns true

QByteArray("").isNull();        // returns false
QByteArray("").isEmpty();       // returns true

QByteArray("abc").isNull();     // returns false
QByteArray("abc").isEmpty();    // returns false
```

All functions except
[isNull](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#isNull)() treat null byte
arrays the same as empty byte arrays. For example,
[data](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#data)() returns a valid
pointer (*not* nullptr) to a '\\\\0' character for a byte array and QByteArray() compares
equal to QByteArray(""). We recommend that you always use
[isEmpty](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#isEmpty)() and avoid
[isNull](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#isNull)().

### Maximum size and out-of-memory conditions

The current version of QByteArray is limited to just under 2 GB (2^31 bytes) in size.
The exact value is architecture-dependent, since it depends on the overhead required for
managing the data block, but is no more than 32 bytes. Raw data blocks are also limited
by the use of `int` type in the current version to 2 GB minus 1 byte.

In case memory allocation fails, QByteArray will throw a `std::bad_alloc` exception. Out
of memory conditions in the Qt containers are the only case where Qt will throw
exceptions.

Note that the operating system may impose further limits on applications holding a lot
of allocated memory, especially large, contiguous blocks. Such considerations, the
configuration of such behavior or any mitigation are outside the scope of the QByteArray
API.

### Notes on Locale

#### Number-String Conversions

Functions that perform conversions between numeric data types and strings are performed
in the C locale, irrespective of the user's locale settings. Use
[QString](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qstring.html) to perform locale-aware
conversions between numbers and strings.

#### 8-bit Character Comparisons

In QByteArray, the notion of uppercase and lowercase and of which character is greater
than or less than another character is done in the Latin-1 locale. This affects
functions that support a case insensitive option or that compare or lowercase or
uppercase their arguments. Case insensitive operations and comparisons will be accurate
if both strings contain only Latin-1 characters. Functions that this affects include
[contains](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#contains)(),
[indexOf](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#indexOf)(),
[lastIndexOf](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#lastIndexOf)(),
operator\\<(), operator\\<\\=(), operator\\>(), operator\\>\\=(),
[isLower](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#isLower)(),
[isUpper](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#isUpper)(),
[toLower](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#toLower)() and
[toUpper](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#toUpper)().

This issue does not apply to [QString](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qstring.html)s
since they represent characters using Unicode.

