### char \\*QByteArray::data()

Returns a pointer to the data stored in the byte array. The pointer can be used to
access and modify the bytes that compose the array. The data is '\\\\0'-terminated, i.e.
the number of bytes in the returned character string is
[size](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#size)() \\+ 1 for the '\\\\0'
terminator.

Example:

```
QByteArray ba("Hello world");
char *data = ba.data();
while (*data) {
    cout << "[" << *data << "]" << Qt::endl;
    ++data;
}
```

The pointer remains valid as long as the byte array isn't reallocated or destroyed. For
read-only access,
[constData](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#constData)() is faster
because it never causes a [deep
copy](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/implicit-sharing.html#deep-copy) to occur.

This function is mostly useful to pass a byte array to a function that accepts a `const
char *`.

The following example makes a copy of the char\\* returned by data(), but it will corrupt
the heap and cause a crash because it does not allocate a byte for the '\\\\0' at the end:

```
QString tmp = "test";
QByteArray text = tmp.toLocal8Bit();
char *data = new char[text.size()];
strcpy(data, text.data());
delete [] data;
```

This one allocates the correct amount of space:

```
QString tmp = "test";
QByteArray text = tmp.toLocal8Bit();
char *data = new char[text.size() + 1];
strcpy(data, text.data());
delete [] data;
```

Note: A [QByteArray](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html) can store any
byte values including '\\\\0's, but most functions that take `char *` arguments assume
that the data ends at the first '\\\\0' they encounter.

**See also** [constData](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#constData)()
and [operator\\[\\]](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbytearray.html#operator-5b-5d)().

