    backgroundBrush: QBrush
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### backgroundBrush : [QBrush](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbrush.html)\n\nThis property holds the background brush of the scene.\n\nSet this property to changes the scene's background to a different color, gradient or\ntexture. The default background brush is\n[Qt::NoBrush](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qt.html#BrushStyle-enum). The\nbackground is drawn before (behind) the items.\n\nExample:\n\n```\nQGraphicsScene scene;\nQGraphicsView view(&scene);\nview.show();\n\n// a blue background\nscene.setBackgroundBrush(Qt::blue);\n\n// a gradient background\nQRadialGradient gradient(0, 0, 10);\ngradient.setSpread(QGradient::RepeatSpread);\nscene.setBackgroundBrush(gradient);\n```\n\n[QGraphicsScene::render](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgraphicsscene.html#render)()\ncalls\n[drawBackground](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgraphicsscene.html#drawBackground)()\nto draw the scene background. For more detailed control over how the background is\ndrawn, you can reimplement\n[drawBackground](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgraphicsscene.html#drawBackground)()\nin a subclass of\n[QGraphicsScene](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgraphicsscene.html).\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| QBrush | **backgroundBrush**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setBackgroundBrush**(const QBrush \\&*brush*) |\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgraphicsscene.html#backgroundBrush"
    bspTreeDepth: int
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### bspTreeDepth : int\n\nThis property holds the depth of\n[QGraphicsScene](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgraphicsscene.html)'s BSP index\ntree\n\nThis property has no effect when\n[NoIndex](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgraphicsscene.html#ItemIndexMethod-enum)\nis used.\n\nThis value determines the depth of\n[QGraphicsScene](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgraphicsscene.html)'s BSP tree. The\ndepth directly affects\n[QGraphicsScene](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgraphicsscene.html)'s performance\nand memory usage; the latter growing exponentially with the depth of the tree. With an\noptimal tree depth,\n[QGraphicsScene](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgraphicsscene.html) can instantly\ndetermine the locality of items, even for scenes with thousands or millions of items.\nThis also greatly improves rendering performance.\n\nBy default, the value is 0, in which case Qt will guess a reasonable default depth based\non the size, location and number of items in the scene. If these parameters change\nfrequently, however, you may experience slowdowns as\n[QGraphicsScene](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgraphicsscene.html) retunes the\ndepth internally. You can avoid potential slowdowns by fixating the tree depth through\nsetting this property.\n\nThe depth of the tree and the size of the scene rectangle decide the granularity of the\nscene's partitioning. The size of each scene segment is determined by the following\nalgorithm:\n\n```\nQSizeF segmentSize = sceneRect().size() / pow(2, depth - 1);\n```\n\nThe BSP tree has an optimal size when each segment contains between 0 and 10 items.\n\nThis property was introduced in Qt 4.3.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| int | **bspTreeDepth**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setBspTreeDepth**(int *depth*) |\n\n**See also**\n[itemIndexMethod](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgraphicsscene.html#itemIndexMethod-prop).\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgraphicsscene.html#bspTreeDepth"
    focusOnTouch: bool
    'Qt 5 reference:\n\n### focusOnTouch : bool\n\nThis property holds whether items gain focus when receiving a *touch begin* event.\n\nThe usual behavior is to transfer focus only when an item is clicked. Often a tap on a\ntouchpad is interpreted as equivalent to a mouse click by the operating system,\ngenerating a synthesized click event in response. However, at least on macOS you can\nconfigure this behavior.\n\nBy default, [QGraphicsScene](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgraphicsscene.html)\nalso transfers focus when you touch on a trackpad or similar. If the operating system is\nconfigured to not generate a synthetic mouse click on tapping the trackpad, this is\nsurprising. If the operating system does generate synthetic mouse clicks on tapping the\ntrackpad, the focus transfer on starting a touch gesture is unnecessary.\n\nWith focusOnTouch switched off,\n[QGraphicsScene](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgraphicsscene.html) behaves as one\nwould expect on macOS.\n\nThe default value is `true`, ensuring that the default behavior is just as in Qt\nversions prior to 5.12. Set to `false` to prevent touch events from triggering focus\nchanges.\n\nThis property was introduced in Qt 5.12.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| bool | **focusOnTouch**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setFocusOnTouch**(bool *enabled*) |\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgraphicsscene.html#focusOnTouch'
    font: QFont
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### font : [QFont](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qfont.html)\n\nThis property holds the scene's default font\n\nThis property provides the scene's font. The scene font defaults to, and resolves all\nits entries from,\n[QApplication::font](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qapplication.html#font).\n\nIf the scene's font changes, either directly through setFont() or indirectly when the\napplication font changes,\n[QGraphicsScene](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgraphicsscene.html) first sends\nitself a [FontChange](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qevent.html#Type-enum) event,\nand it then sends [FontChange](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qevent.html#Type-enum)\nevents to all top-level widget items in the scene. These items respond by resolving\ntheir own fonts to the scene, and they then notify their children, who again notify\ntheir children, and so on, until all widget items have updated their fonts.\n\nChanging the scene font, (directly or indirectly through\n[QApplication::setFont](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qapplication.html#setFont)(),)\nautomatically schedules a redraw the entire scene.\n\nThis property was introduced in Qt 4.4.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| QFont | **font**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setFont**(const QFont \\&*font*) |\n\n**See also** [QWidget::font](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#font-prop),\n[QApplication::setFont](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qapplication.html#setFont)(),\n[palette](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgraphicsscene.html#palette-prop), and\n[style](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgraphicsscene.html#style)().\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgraphicsscene.html#font"
    foregroundBrush: QBrush
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### foregroundBrush : [QBrush](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qbrush.html)\n\nThis property holds the foreground brush of the scene.\n\nChange this property to set the scene's foreground to a different color, gradient or\ntexture.\n\nThe foreground is drawn after (on top of) the items. The default foreground brush is\n[Qt::NoBrush](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qt.html#BrushStyle-enum) ( i.e. the\nforeground is not drawn).\n\nExample:\n\n```\nQGraphicsScene scene;\nQGraphicsView view(&scene);\nview.show();\n\n// a white semi-transparent foreground\nscene.setForegroundBrush(QColor(255, 255, 255, 127));\n\n// a grid foreground\nscene.setForegroundBrush(QBrush(Qt::lightGray, Qt::CrossPattern));\n```\n\n[QGraphicsScene::render](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgraphicsscene.html#render)()\ncalls\n[drawForeground](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgraphicsscene.html#drawForeground)()\nto draw the scene foreground. For more detailed control over how the foreground is\ndrawn, you can reimplement the\n[drawForeground](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgraphicsscene.html#drawForeground)()\nfunction in a [QGraphicsScene](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgraphicsscene.html)\nsubclass.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| QBrush | **foregroundBrush**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setForegroundBrush**(const QBrush \\&*brush*) |\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgraphicsscene.html#foregroundBrush"
    itemIndexMethod: Any
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### itemIndexMethod : [ItemIndexMethod](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgraphicsscene.html#ItemIndexMethod-enum)\n\nThis property holds the item indexing method.\n\n[QGraphicsScene](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgraphicsscene.html) applies an\nindexing algorithm to the scene, to speed up item discovery functions like\n[items](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgraphicsscene.html#items)() and\n[itemAt](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgraphicsscene.html#itemAt)(). Indexing is\nmost efficient for static scenes (i.e., where items don't move around). For dynamic\nscenes, or scenes with many animated items, the index bookkeeping can outweigh the fast\nlookup speeds.\n\nFor the common case, the default index method\n[BspTreeIndex](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgraphicsscene.html#ItemIndexMethod-enum)\nworks fine. If your scene uses many animations and you are experiencing slowness, you\ncan disable indexing by calling `setItemIndexMethod(NoIndex)`.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| QGraphicsScene::ItemIndexMethod | **itemIndexMethod**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setItemIndexMethod**(QGraphicsScene::ItemIndexMethod *method*) |\n\n**See also**\n[bspTreeDepth](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgraphicsscene.html#bspTreeDepth-prop).\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgraphicsscene.html#itemIndexMethod"
    minimumRenderSize: float
    'Qt 5 reference:\n\n### minimumRenderSize : [qreal](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qtglobal.html#qreal-typedef)\n\nThis property holds the minimal view-transformed size an item must have to be drawn\n\nWhen the scene is rendered, any item whose width or height, transformed to the target\nview, is smaller that minimumRenderSize(), will not be rendered. If an item is not\nrendered and it clips its children items they will also not be rendered. Set this value\nto speed up rendering of scenes with many objects rendered on a zoomed out view.\n\nThe default value is 0\\. If unset, or if set to 0 or a negative value, all items will\nalways be rendered.\n\nFor example, setting this property can be especially useful if a scene is rendered by\nmultiple views, one of which serves as an overview which always displays all items. In\nscenes with many items, such a view will use a high scaling factor so that all items can\nbe shown. Due to the scaling, smaller items will only make an insignificant contribution\nto the final rendered scene. To avoid drawing these items and reduce the time necessary\nto render the scene, you can call setMinimumRenderSize() with a non-negative value.\n\n**Note:** Items that are not drawn as a result of being too small, are still returned by\nmethods such as [items](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgraphicsscene.html#items)()\nand [itemAt](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgraphicsscene.html#itemAt)(), and\nparticipate in collision detection and interactions. It is recommended that you set\nminimumRenderSize() to a value less than or equal to 1 in order to avoid large\nunrendered items that are interactive.\n\nThis property was introduced in Qt 5.4.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| qreal | **minimumRenderSize**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setMinimumRenderSize**(qreal *minSize*) |\n\n**See also**\n[QStyleOptionGraphicsItem::levelOfDetailFromTransform](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qstyleoptiongraphicsitem.html#levelOfDetailFromTransform)().\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgraphicsscene.html#minimumRenderSize'
    objectName: Any
    palette: QPalette
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### palette : [QPalette](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qpalette.html)\n\nThis property holds the scene's default palette\n\nThis property provides the scene's palette. The scene palette defaults to, and resolves\nall its entries from,\n[QApplication::palette](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qapplication.html#palette).\n\nIf the scene's palette changes, either directly through setPalette() or indirectly when\nthe application palette changes,\n[QGraphicsScene](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgraphicsscene.html) first sends\nitself a [PaletteChange](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qevent.html#Type-enum)\nevent, and it then sends\n[PaletteChange](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qevent.html#Type-enum) events to all\ntop-level widget items in the scene. These items respond by resolving their own palettes\nto the scene, and they then notify their children, who again notify their children, and\nso on, until all widget items have updated their palettes.\n\nChanging the scene palette, (directly or indirectly through\n[QApplication::setPalette](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qapplication.html#setPalette)(),)\nautomatically schedules a redraw the entire scene.\n\nThis property was introduced in Qt 4.4.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| QPalette | **palette**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setPalette**(const QPalette \\&*palette*) |\n\n**See also**\n[QWidget::palette](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#palette-prop),\n[QApplication::setPalette](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qapplication.html#setPalette)(),\n[font](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/stylesheet-reference.html#font), and\n[style](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgraphicsscene.html#style)().\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgraphicsscene.html#palette"
