class QGridLayout:
    """PythonQtInstanceWrapper object

Native bases: PythonQtInstanceWrapper

Supplemental documentation:
Qt 5 reference:

## Detailed Description

QGridLayout takes the space made available to it (by its parent layout or by the
[parentWidget](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qlayout.html#parentWidget)()), divides
it up into rows and columns, and puts each widget it manages into the correct cell.

Columns and rows behave identically; we will discuss columns, but there are equivalent
functions for rows.

Each column has a minimum width and a stretch factor. The minimum width is the greatest
of that set using
[setColumnMinimumWidth](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgridlayout.html#setColumnMinimumWidth)()
and the minimum width of each widget in that column. The stretch factor is set using
[setColumnStretch](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgridlayout.html#setColumnStretch)()
and determines how much of the available space the column will get over and above its
necessary minimum.

Normally, each managed widget or layout is put into a cell of its own using
[addWidget](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgridlayout.html#addWidget-1)(). It is
also possible for a widget to occupy multiple cells using the row and column spanning
overloads of [addItem](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgridlayout.html#addItem)()
and [addWidget](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgridlayout.html#addWidget-1)(). If
you do this, QGridLayout will guess how to distribute the size over the columns/rows
(based on the stretch factors).

To remove a widget from a layout, call
[removeWidget](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qlayout.html#removeWidget)(). Calling
[QWidget::hide](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#hide)() on a widget also
effectively removes the widget from the layout until
[QWidget::show](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#show)() is called.

This illustration shows a fragment of a dialog with a five-column, three-row grid (the
grid is shown overlaid in magenta):

![A grid layout](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/images/qgridlayout.png)

Columns 0, 2 and 4 in this dialog fragment are made up of a
[QLabel](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qlabel.html), a
[QLineEdit](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qlineedit.html), and a QListBox. Columns
1 and 3 are placeholders made with
[setColumnMinimumWidth](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgridlayout.html#setColumnMinimumWidth)().
Row 0 consists of three [QLabel](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qlabel.html)
objects, row 1 of three [QLineEdit](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qlineedit.html)
objects and row 2 of three QListBox objects. We used placeholder columns (1 and 3\\) to
get the right amount of space between the columns.

Note that the columns and rows are not equally wide or tall. If you want two columns to
have the same width, you must set their minimum widths and stretch factors to be the
same yourself. You do this using
[setColumnMinimumWidth](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgridlayout.html#setColumnMinimumWidth)()
and
[setColumnStretch](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgridlayout.html#setColumnStretch)().

If the QGridLayout is not the top-level layout (i.e. does not manage all of the widget's
area and children), you must add it to its parent layout when you create it, but before
you do anything with it. The normal way to add a layout is by calling
[addLayout](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgridlayout.html#addLayout)() on the
parent layout.

Once you have added your layout you can start putting widgets and other layouts into the
cells of your grid layout using
[addWidget](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgridlayout.html#addWidget-1)(),
[addItem](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgridlayout.html#addItem)(), and
[addLayout](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgridlayout.html#addLayout)().

QGridLayout also includes two margin widths: the [contents
margin](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qlayout.html#getContentsMargins) and the
[spacing](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgridlayout.html#spacing)(). The contents
margin is the width of the reserved space along each of the QGridLayout's four sides.
The [spacing](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgridlayout.html#spacing)() is the
width of the automatically allocated spacing between neighboring boxes.

The default contents margin values are provided by the
[style](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qstyle.html#pixelMetric). The default value
Qt styles specify is 9 for child widgets and 11 for windows. The spacing defaults to the
same as the margin width for a top-level layout, or to the same as the parent layout.

Source: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgridlayout.html"""

