QMessageLogger is used to generate messages for the Qt logging framework. Usually one
uses it through [qDebug](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qtglobal.html#qDebug)(),
[qInfo](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qtglobal.html#qInfo)(),
[qWarning](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qtglobal.html#qWarning)(),
[qCritical](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qtglobal.html#qCritical), or
[qFatal](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qtglobal.html#qFatal)() functions, which are
actually macros: For example
[qDebug](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qtglobal.html#qDebug)() expands to
QMessageLogger(\\_\\_FILE\\_\\_, \\_\\_LINE\\_\\_,
[Q\\_FUNC\\_INFO](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qtglobal.html#Q_FUNC_INFO)).[debug](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qmessagelogger.html#debug-3)()
for debug builds, and QMessageLogger(0, 0,
0).[debug](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qmessagelogger.html#debug-3)() for release
builds.

One example of direct use is to forward errors that stem from a scripting language, e.g.
QML:

```
    void statusChanged(QDeclarativeComponent::Status status) {
        if (status == QDeclarativeComponent::Error) {
            foreach (const QDeclarativeError &error, component->errors()) {
                const QByteArray file = error.url().toEncoded();
                QMessageLogger(file.constData(), error.line(), 0).debug() << error.description();
            }
        }
    }
```

