class QPaintEngine:
    """PythonQtInstanceWrapper object

Native bases: PythonQtInstanceWrapper

Supplemental documentation:
Qt 5 reference:

## Detailed Description

Qt provides several premade implementations of QPaintEngine for the different painter
backends we support. The primary paint engine provided is the raster paint engine, which
contains a software rasterizer which supports the full feature set on all supported
platforms. This is the default for painting on
[QWidget](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html)-based classes in e.g. on
Windows, X11 and macOS, it is the backend for painting on
[QImage](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qimage.html) and it is used as a fallback
for paint engines that do not support a certain capability. In addition we provide
QPaintEngine implementations for OpenGL (accessible through
[QOpenGLWidget](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qopenglwidget.html)) and printing
(which allows using [QPainter](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qpainter.html) to draw
on a [QPrinter](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qprinter.html) object).

If one wants to use [QPainter](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qpainter.html) to draw
to a different backend, one must subclass QPaintEngine and reimplement all its virtual
functions. The QPaintEngine implementation is then made available by subclassing
[QPaintDevice](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qpaintdevice.html) and reimplementing
the virtual function
[QPaintDevice::paintEngine](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qpaintdevice.html#paintEngine)().

QPaintEngine is created and owned by the
[QPaintDevice](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qpaintdevice.html) that created it.

Source: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qpaintengine.html"""

