class QProxyStyle:
    """PythonQtInstanceWrapper object

Native bases: PythonQtInstanceWrapper

Supplemental documentation:
Qt 5 reference:

## Detailed Description

A QProxyStyle wraps a [QStyle](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qstyle.html) (usually
the default system style) for the purpose of dynamically overriding painting or other
specific style behavior.

The following example shows how to override the shortcut underline behavior on any
platform:

```
#include "textedit.h"
#include <QApplication>
#include <QProxyStyle>

class MyProxyStyle : public QProxyStyle
{
  public:
    int styleHint(StyleHint hint, const QStyleOption *option = nullptr,
                  const QWidget *widget = nullptr, QStyleHintReturn *returnData = nullptr) const override
    {
        if (hint == QStyle::SH_UnderlineShortcut)
            return 0;
        return QProxyStyle::styleHint(hint, option, widget, returnData);
    }
};

int main(int argc, char **argv)
{
    Q_INIT_RESOURCE(textedit);

    QApplication a(argc, argv);
    a.setStyle(new MyProxyStyle);
    TextEdit mw;
    mw.resize(700, 800);
    mw.show();
    //...
}
```

Warning: The [common styles](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qcommonstyle.html)
provided by Qt will respect this hint, because they call
[QStyle::proxy](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qstyle.html#proxy)(), but there is no
guarantee that [QStyle::proxy](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qstyle.html#proxy)()
will be called for user defined or system controlled styles. It would not work on a Mac,
for example, where menus are handled by the operating system.

