### bool QRegExp::exactMatch(const [QString](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qstring.html) \\&*str*) const

Returns `true` if *str* is matched exactly by this regular expression; otherwise returns
`false`. You can determine how much of the string was matched by calling
[matchedLength](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qregexp.html#matchedLength)().

For a given regexp string R, exactMatch("R") is the equivalent of
[indexIn](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qregexp.html#indexIn)("^R$") since
exactMatch() effectively encloses the regexp in the start of string and end of string
anchors, except that it sets
[matchedLength](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qregexp.html#matchedLength)()
differently.

For example, if the regular expression is **blue**, then exactMatch() returns `true`
only for input `blue`. For inputs `bluebell`, `blutak` and `lightblue`, exactMatch()
returns `false` and
[matchedLength](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qregexp.html#matchedLength)() will
return 4, 3 and 0 respectively.

Although const, this function sets
[matchedLength](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qregexp.html#matchedLength)(),
[capturedTexts](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qregexp.html#capturedTexts)(), and
[pos](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qregexp.html#pos)().

