Qt 5 reference:

## Detailed Description

A QRegularExpressionMatch object can be obtained by calling the
[QRegularExpression::match](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qregularexpression.html#match)()
function, or as a single result of a global match from a
[QRegularExpressionMatchIterator](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qregularexpressionmatchiterator.html).

The success or the failure of a match attempt can be inspected by calling the
[hasMatch](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qregularexpressionmatch.html#hasMatch)()
function. QRegularExpressionMatch also reports a successful partial match through the
[hasPartialMatch](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qregularexpressionmatch.html#hasPartialMatch)()
function.

In addition, QRegularExpressionMatch returns the substrings captured by the capturing
groups in the pattern string. The implicit capturing group with index 0 captures the
result of the whole match. The
[captured](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qregularexpressionmatch.html#captured)()
function returns each substring captured, either by the capturing group's index or by
its name:

```
QRegularExpression re("(\\\\d\\\\d) (?<name>\\\\w+)");
QRegularExpressionMatch match = re.match("23 Jordan");
if (match.hasMatch()) {
    QString number = match.captured(1); // first == "23"
    QString name = match.captured("name"); // name == "Jordan"
}
```

For each captured substring it is possible to query its starting and ending offsets in
the subject string by calling the
[capturedStart](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qregularexpressionmatch.html#capturedStart)()
and the
[capturedEnd](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qregularexpressionmatch.html#capturedEnd)()
function, respectively. The length of each captured substring is available using the
[capturedLength](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qregularexpressionmatch.html#capturedLength)()
function.

The convenience function
[capturedTexts](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qregularexpressionmatch.html#capturedTexts)()
will return *all* the captured substrings at once (including the substring matched by
the entire pattern) in the order they have been captured by capturing groups; that is,
`captured(i) == capturedTexts().at(i)`.

You can retrieve the
[QRegularExpression](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qregularexpression.html) object
the subject string was matched against by calling the
[regularExpression](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qregularexpressionmatch.html#regularExpression)()
function; the match type and the match options are available as well by calling the
[matchType](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qregularexpressionmatch.html#matchType)()
and the
[matchOptions](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qregularexpressionmatch.html#matchOptions)()
respectively.

Please refer to the
[QRegularExpression](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qregularexpression.html)
documentation for more information about the Qt regular expression classes.

