class QRubberBand:
    """PythonQtInstanceWrapper object

Native bases: PythonQtInstanceWrapper

Supplemental documentation:
Qt 5 reference:

## Detailed Description

A rubber band is often used to show a new bounding area (as in a
[QSplitter](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qsplitter.html) or a
[QDockWidget](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qdockwidget.html) that is undocking).
Historically this has been implemented using a
[QPainter](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qpainter.html) and XOR, but this approach
doesn't always work properly since rendering can happen in the window below the rubber
band, but before the rubber band has been "erased".

You can create a QRubberBand whenever you need to render a rubber band around a given
area (or to represent a single line), then call
[setGeometry](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qrubberband.html#setGeometry)(),
[move](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qrubberband.html#move)() or
[resize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qrubberband.html#resize)() to position and
size it. A common pattern is to do this in conjunction with mouse events. For example:

```
void Widget::mousePressEvent(QMouseEvent *event)
{
    origin = event->pos();
    if (!rubberBand)
        rubberBand = new QRubberBand(QRubberBand::Rectangle, this);
    rubberBand->setGeometry(QRect(origin, QSize()));
    rubberBand->show();
}

void Widget::mouseMoveEvent(QMouseEvent *event)
{
    rubberBand->setGeometry(QRect(origin, event->pos()).normalized());
}

void Widget::mouseReleaseEvent(QMouseEvent *event)
{
    rubberBand->hide();
    // determine selection, for example using QRect::intersects()
    // and QRect::contains().
}
```

If you pass a parent to QRubberBand's constructor, the rubber band will display only
inside its parent, but stays on top of other child widgets. If no parent is passed,
QRubberBand will act as a top-level widget.

Call [show](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#show)() to make the rubber
band visible; also when the rubber band is not a top-level. Hiding or destroying the
widget will make the rubber band disappear. The rubber band can be a
[Rectangle](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qrubberband.html#Shape-enum) or a
[Line](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qrubberband.html#Shape-enum) (vertical or
horizontal), depending on the
[shape](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qrubberband.html#shape)() it was given when
constructed.

Source: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qrubberband.html"""

