You can use [QThreadPool](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qthreadpool.html) to
execute your code in a separate thread.
[QThreadPool](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qthreadpool.html) deletes the QRunnable
automatically if
[autoDelete](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qrunnable.html#autoDelete)() returns
`true` (the default). Use
[setAutoDelete](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qrunnable.html#setAutoDelete)() to
change the auto-deletion flag.

[QThreadPool](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qthreadpool.html) supports executing
the same QRunnable more than once by calling QThreadPool::tryStart(this) from within the
[run](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qrunnable.html#run)() function. If
[autoDelete](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qrunnable.html#autoDelete) is enabled
the QRunnable will be deleted when the last thread exits the run function. Calling
[QThreadPool::start](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qthreadpool.html#start)()
multiple times with the same QRunnable when
[autoDelete](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qrunnable.html#autoDelete) is enabled
creates a race condition and is not recommended.

