    """PythonQtInstanceWrapper object

Native bases: PythonQtInstanceWrapper

Supplemental documentation:
Qt 5 reference:

## Detailed Description

Wheel events are sent to the widget under the mouse cursor, but if that widget does not
handle the event they are sent to the focus widget. Wheel events are generated for both
mouse wheels and trackpad scroll gestures. There are two ways to read the wheel event
delta: [angleDelta](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwheelevent.html#angleDelta)()
returns the deltas in wheel degrees. These values are always provided.
[pixelDelta](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwheelevent.html#pixelDelta)() returns
the deltas in screen pixels, and is available on platforms that have high-resolution
trackpads, such as macOS. If that is the case,
[source](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwheelevent.html#source)() will return
[Qt::MouseEventSynthesizedBySystem](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qt.html#MouseEventSource-enum).

The functions pos() and globalPos() return the mouse cursor's location at the time of
the event.

A wheel event contains a special accept flag that indicates whether the receiver wants
the event. You should call
[ignore](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qevent.html#ignore)() if you do not handle
the wheel event; this ensures that it will be sent to the parent widget.

The
[QWidget::setEnabled](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#enabled-prop)()
function can be used to enable or disable mouse and keyboard events for a widget.

The event handler
[QWidget::wheelEvent](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#wheelEvent)()
receives wheel events.

Source: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwheelevent.html"""

