### `[static]` [QWidget](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#QWidget) \\*QWidget::createWindowContainer([QWindow](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwindow.html) \\**window*, [QWidget](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#QWidget) \\**parent* \\= nullptr, [Qt::WindowFlags](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qt.html#WindowType-enum) *flags* \\= Qt::WindowFlags())

Creates a [QWidget](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html) that makes it
possible to embed *window* into a
[QWidget](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html)-based application.

The window container is created as a child of *parent* and with window flags *flags*.

Once the window has been embedded into the container, the container will control the
window's geometry and visibility. Explicit calls to
[QWindow::setGeometry](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwindow.html#setGeometry)(),
[QWindow::show](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwindow.html#show)() or
[QWindow::hide](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwindow.html#hide)() on an embedded
window is not recommended.

The container takes over ownership of *window*. The window can be removed from the
window container with a call to
[QWindow::setParent](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwindow.html#setParent)().

The window container is attached as a native child window to the toplevel window it is a
child of. When a window container is used as a child of a
[QAbstractScrollArea](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qabstractscrollarea.html) or
[QMdiArea](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qmdiarea.html), it will create a [native
window](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#native-widgets-vs-alien-widgets)
for every widget in its parent chain to allow for proper stacking and clipping in this
use case. Creating a native window for the window container also allows for proper
stacking and clipping. This must be done before showing the window container.
Applications with many native child windows may suffer from performance issues.

The window container has a number of known limitations:

- Stacking order; The embedded window will stack on top of the widget hierarchy as an opaque box. The stacking order of multiple overlapping window container instances is undefined.
- Rendering Integration; The window container does not interoperate with [QGraphicsProxyWidget](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qgraphicsproxywidget.html), [QWidget::render](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#render)() or similar functionality.
- Focus Handling; It is possible to let the window container instance have any focus policy and it will delegate focus to the window via a call to [QWindow::requestActivate](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwindow.html#requestActivate)(). However, returning to the normal focus chain from the [QWindow](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwindow.html) instance will be up to the [QWindow](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwindow.html) instance implementation itself. For instance, when entering a Qt Quick based window with tab focus, it is quite likely that further tab presses will only cycle inside the QML application. Also, whether [QWindow::requestActivate](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwindow.html#requestActivate)() actually gives the window focus, is platform dependent.
- Using many window container instances in a [QWidget](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html)-based application can greatly hurt the overall performance of the application.

