    palette: QPalette
    'Qt 5 reference:\n\n### palette : [QPalette](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qpalette.html)\n\nThis property holds the widget\'s palette\n\nThis property describes the widget\'s palette. The palette is used by the widget\'s style\nwhen rendering standard components, and is available as a means to ensure that custom\nwidgets can maintain consistency with the native platform\'s look and feel. It\'s common\nthat different platforms, or different styles, have different palettes.\n\nWhen you assign a new palette to a widget, the color roles from this palette are\ncombined with the widget\'s default palette to form the widget\'s final palette. The\npalette entry for the widget\'s background role is used to fill the widget\'s background\n(see\n[QWidget::autoFillBackground](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#autoFillBackground-prop)),\nand the foreground role initializes\n[QPainter](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qpainter.html)\'s pen.\n\nThe default depends on the system environment.\n[QApplication](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qapplication.html) maintains a\nsystem/theme palette which serves as a default for all widgets. There may also be\nspecial palette defaults for certain types of widgets (e.g., on Windows Vista, all\nclasses that derive from [QMenuBar](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qmenubar.html)\nhave a special default palette). You can also define default palettes for widgets\nyourself by passing a custom palette and the name of a widget to\n[QApplication::setPalette](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qapplication.html#setPalette)().\nFinally, the style always has the option of polishing the palette as it\'s assigned (see\n[QStyle::polish](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qstyle.html#polish)()).\n\n[QWidget](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html) propagates explicit palette\nroles from parent to child. If you assign a brush or color to a specific role on a\npalette and assign that palette to a widget, that role will propagate to all the\nwidget\'s children, overriding any system defaults for that role. Note that palettes by\ndefault don\'t propagate to windows (see\n[isWindow](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#isWindow)()) unless the\n[Qt::WA\\_WindowPropagation](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qt.html#WidgetAttribute-enum)\nattribute is enabled.\n\n[QWidget](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html)\'s palette propagation is\nsimilar to its font propagation.\n\nThe current style, which is used to render the content of all standard Qt widgets, is\nfree to choose colors and brushes from the widget palette, or in some cases, to ignore\nthe palette (partially, or completely). In particular, certain styles like GTK style,\nMac style, and Windows Vista style, depend on third party APIs to render the content of\nwidgets, and these styles typically do not follow the palette. Because of this,\nassigning roles to a widget\'s palette is not guaranteed to change the appearance of the\nwidget. Instead, you may choose to apply a\n[styleSheet](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/stylesheet.html).\n\n**Warning:** Do not use this function in conjunction with [Qt Style\nSheets](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/stylesheet.html). When using style sheets,\nthe palette of a widget can be customized using the "color", "background-color",\n"selection-color", "selection-background-color" and "alternate-background-color".\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| const QPalette \\& | **palette**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setPalette**(*const QPalette \\&*) |\n\n**See also**\n[QGuiApplication::palette](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qguiapplication.html#palette)(),\n[QWidget::font](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#font-prop)(), and [Qt\nStyle Sheets](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/stylesheet.html).\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#palette'
    pos: QPoint
    'Qt 5 reference:\n\n### pos : [QPoint](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qpoint.html)\n\nThis property holds the position of the widget within its parent widget\n\nIf the widget is a window, the position is that of the widget on the desktop, including\nits frame.\n\nWhen changing the position, the widget, if visible, receives a move event\n([moveEvent](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#moveEvent)()) immediately.\nIf the widget is not currently visible, it is guaranteed to receive an event before it\nis shown.\n\nBy default, this property contains a position that refers to the origin.\n\n**Warning:** Calling move() or\n[setGeometry](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#geometry-prop)() inside\n[moveEvent](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#moveEvent)() can lead to\ninfinite recursion.\n\nSee the [Window\nGeometry](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/application-windows.html#window-geometry)\ndocumentation for an overview of geometry issues with windows.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| QPoint | **pos**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **[move](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#move-1)**(int *x*, int *y*) |\n| void | **move**(*const QPoint \\&*) |\n\n**See also**\n[frameGeometry](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#frameGeometry-prop),\n[size](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#size-prop),\n[x](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#x-prop)(), and\n[y](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#y-prop)().\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#pos'
    rect: QRect
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### rect : const [QRect](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qrect.html)\n\nThis property holds the internal geometry of the widget excluding any window frame\n\nThe rect property equals [QRect](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qrect.html)(0, 0,\n[width](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#width-prop)(),\n[height](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#height-prop)()).\n\nSee the [Window\nGeometry](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/application-windows.html#window-geometry)\ndocumentation for an overview of geometry issues with windows.\n\nBy default, this property contains a value that depends on the user's platform and\nscreen geometry.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| QRect | **rect**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n\n**See also** [size](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#size-prop).\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#rect"
    size: QSize
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### size : [QSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qsize.html)\n\nThis property holds the size of the widget excluding any window frame\n\nIf the widget is visible when it is being resized, it receives a resize event\n([resizeEvent](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#resizeEvent)())\nimmediately. If the widget is not currently visible, it is guaranteed to receive an\nevent before it is shown.\n\nThe size is adjusted if it lies outside the range defined by\n[minimumSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#minimumSize-prop)() and\n[maximumSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#maximumSize-prop)().\n\nBy default, this property contains a value that depends on the user's platform and\nscreen geometry.\n\n**Warning:** Calling resize() or\n[setGeometry](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#geometry-prop)() inside\n[resizeEvent](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#resizeEvent)() can lead to\ninfinite recursion.\n\n**Note:** Setting the size to `QSize(0, 0)` will cause the widget to not appear on\nscreen. This also applies to windows.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| QSize | **size**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **[resize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#resize-1)**(int *w*, int *h*) |\n| void | **resize**(*const QSize \\&*) |\n\n**See also** [pos](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#pos-prop),\n[geometry](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#geometry-prop),\n[minimumSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#minimumSize-prop),\n[maximumSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#maximumSize-prop),\n[resizeEvent](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#resizeEvent)(), and\n[adjustSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#adjustSize)().\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#size"
    sizeHint: QSize
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### sizeHint : const [QSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qsize.html)\n\nThis property holds the recommended size for the widget\n\nIf the value of this property is an invalid size, no size is recommended.\n\nThe default implementation of sizeHint() returns an invalid size if there is no layout\nfor this widget, and returns the layout's preferred size otherwise.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| virtual QSize | **sizeHint**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n\n**See also** [QSize::isValid](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qsize.html#isValid)(),\n[minimumSizeHint](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#minimumSizeHint-prop)(),\n[sizePolicy](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#sizePolicy-prop)(),\n[setMinimumSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#minimumSize-prop)(),\nand [updateGeometry](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#updateGeometry)().\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#sizeHint"
    sizeIncrement: QSize
    'Qt 5 reference:\n\n### sizeIncrement : [QSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qsize.html)\n\nThis property holds the size increment of the widget\n\nWhen the user resizes the window, the size will move in steps of\nsizeIncrement().[width](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#width-prop)()\npixels horizontally and\nsizeIncrement.[height](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#height-prop)()\npixels vertically, with\n[baseSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#baseSize-prop)() as the\nbasis. Preferred widget sizes are for non-negative integers *i* and *j*:\n\n```\nwidth = baseSize().width() + i * sizeIncrement().width();\nheight = baseSize().height() + j * sizeIncrement().height();\n```\n\nNote that while you can set the size increment for all widgets, it only affects windows.\n\nBy default, this property contains a size with zero width and height.\n\n**Warning:** The size increment has no effect under Windows, and may be disregarded by\nthe window manager on X11.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| QSize | **sizeIncrement**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setSizeIncrement**(*const QSize \\&*) |\n| void | **[setSizeIncrement](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#setSizeIncrement-1)**(int *w*, int *h*) |\n\n**See also** [size](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#size-prop),\n[minimumSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#minimumSize-prop), and\n[maximumSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#maximumSize-prop).\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#sizeIncrement'
    sizePolicy: QSizePolicy
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### sizePolicy : [QSizePolicy](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qsizepolicy.html)\n\nThis property holds the default layout behavior of the widget\n\nIf there is a [QLayout](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qlayout.html) that manages\nthis widget's children, the size policy specified by that layout is used. If there is no\nsuch [QLayout](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qlayout.html), the result of this\nfunction is used.\n\nThe default policy is Preferred/Preferred, which means that the widget can be freely\nresized, but prefers to be the size\n[sizeHint](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#sizeHint-prop)() returns.\nButton-like widgets set the size policy to specify that they may stretch horizontally,\nbut are fixed vertically. The same applies to lineedit controls (such as\n[QLineEdit](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qlineedit.html),\n[QSpinBox](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qspinbox.html) or an editable\n[QComboBox](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qcombobox.html)) and other horizontally\norientated widgets (such as\n[QProgressBar](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qprogressbar.html)).\n[QToolButton](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qtoolbutton.html)'s are normally\nsquare, so they allow growth in both directions. Widgets that support different\ndirections (such as [QSlider](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qslider.html),\n[QScrollBar](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qscrollbar.html) or QHeader) specify\nstretching in the respective direction only. Widgets that can provide scroll bars\n(usually subclasses of\n[QScrollArea](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qscrollarea.html)) tend to specify that\nthey can use additional space, and that they can make do with less than\n[sizeHint](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#sizeHint-prop)().\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| QSizePolicy | **sizePolicy**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setSizePolicy**(*QSizePolicy*) |\n| void | **[setSizePolicy](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#setSizePolicy-1)**(QSizePolicy::Policy *horizontal*, QSizePolicy::Policy *vertical*) |\n\n**See also**\n[sizeHint](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#sizeHint-prop)(),\n[QLayout](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qlayout.html),\n[QSizePolicy](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qsizepolicy.html), and\n[updateGeometry](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#updateGeometry)().\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#sizePolicy"
    statusTip: str
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### statusTip : [QString](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qstring.html)\n\nThis property holds the widget's status tip\n\nBy default, this property contains an empty string.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| QString | **statusTip**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setStatusTip**(*const QString \\&*) |\n\n**See also** [toolTip](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#toolTip-prop) and\n[whatsThis](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#whatsThis-prop).\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#statusTip"
    styleSheet: str
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### styleSheet : [QString](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qstring.html)\n\nThis property holds the widget's style sheet\n\nThe style sheet contains a textual description of customizations to the widget's style,\nas described in the [Qt Style\nSheets](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/stylesheet.html) document.\n\nSince Qt 4.5, Qt style sheets fully supports macOS.\n\n**Warning:** Qt style sheets are currently not supported for custom\n[QStyle](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qstyle.html) subclasses. We plan to address\nthis in some future release.\n\nThis property was introduced in Qt 4.2.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| QString | **styleSheet**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setStyleSheet**(const QString \\&*styleSheet*) |\n\n**See also** [setStyle](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#setStyle)(),\n[QApplication::styleSheet](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qapplication.html#styleSheet-prop),\nand [Qt Style Sheets](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/stylesheet.html).\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#styleSheet"
    tabletTracking: Any
    'Qt 5 reference:\n\n### tabletTracking : bool\n\nThis property holds whether tablet tracking is enabled for the widget\n\nIf tablet tracking is disabled (the default), the widget only receives tablet move\nevents when the stylus is in contact with the tablet, or at least one stylus button is\npressed, while the stylus is being moved.\n\nIf tablet tracking is enabled, the widget receives tablet move events even while\nhovering in proximity. This is useful for monitoring position as well as the auxiliary\nproperties such as rotation and tilt, and providing feedback in the UI.\n\nThis property was introduced in Qt 5.9.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| bool | **hasTabletTracking**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setTabletTracking**(bool *enable*) |\n\n**See also**\n[tabletEvent](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#tabletEvent)().\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#tabletTracking'
    toolTip: str
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### toolTip : [QString](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qstring.html)\n\nThis property holds the widget's tooltip\n\nNote that by default tooltips are only shown for widgets that are children of the active\nwindow. You can change this behavior by setting the attribute\n[Qt::WA\\_AlwaysShowToolTips](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qt.html#WidgetAttribute-enum)\non the *window*, not on the widget with the tooltip.\n\nIf you want to control a tooltip's behavior, you can intercept the\n[event](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#event)() function and catch the\n[QEvent::ToolTip](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qevent.html#Type-enum) event (e.g.,\nif you want to customize the area for which the tooltip should be shown).\n\nBy default, this property contains an empty string.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| QString | **toolTip**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setToolTip**(*const QString \\&*) |\n\n**See also** [QToolTip](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qtooltip.html),\n[statusTip](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#statusTip-prop), and\n[whatsThis](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#whatsThis-prop).\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#toolTip"
    toolTipDuration: int
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### toolTipDuration : int\n\nThis property holds the widget's tooltip duration\n\nSpecifies how long time the tooltip will be displayed, in milliseconds. If the value is\n-1 (default) the duration is calculated depending on the length of the tooltip.\n\nThis property was introduced in Qt 5.2.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| int | **toolTipDuration**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setToolTipDuration**(int *msec*) |\n\n**See also** [toolTip](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#toolTip-prop).\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#toolTipDuration"
    updatesEnabled: bool
    'Qt 5 reference:\n\n### updatesEnabled : bool\n\nThis property holds whether updates are enabled\n\nAn updates enabled widget receives paint events and has a system background; a disabled\nwidget does not. This also implies that calling\n[update](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#update)() and\n[repaint](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#repaint)() has no effect if\nupdates are disabled.\n\nBy default, this property is `true`.\n\nsetUpdatesEnabled() is normally used to disable updates for a short period of time, for\ninstance to avoid screen flicker during large changes. In Qt, widgets normally do not\ngenerate screen flicker, but on X11 the server might erase regions on the screen when\nwidgets get hidden before they can be replaced by other widgets. Disabling updates\nsolves this.\n\nExample:\n\n```\nsetUpdatesEnabled(false);\nbigVisualChanges();\nsetUpdatesEnabled(true);\n```\n\nDisabling a widget implicitly disables all its children. Enabling a widget enables all\nchild widgets *except* top-level widgets or those that have been explicitly disabled.\nRe-enabling updates implicitly calls\n[update](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#update)() on the widget.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| bool | **updatesEnabled**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setUpdatesEnabled**(bool *enable*) |\n\n**See also** [paintEvent](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#paintEvent)().\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#updatesEnabled'
    visible: Any
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### visible : bool\n\nThis property holds whether the widget is visible\n\nCalling setVisible(true) or\n[show](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#show)() sets the widget to\nvisible status if all its parent widgets up to the window are visible. If an ancestor is\nnot visible, the widget won't become visible until all its ancestors are shown. If its\nsize or position has changed, Qt guarantees that a widget gets move and resize events\njust before it is shown. If the widget has not been resized yet, Qt will adjust the\nwidget's size to a useful default using\n[adjustSize](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#adjustSize)().\n\nCalling setVisible(false) or\n[hide](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#hide)() hides a widget\nexplicitly. An explicitly hidden widget will never become visible, even if all its\nancestors become visible, unless you show it.\n\nA widget receives show and hide events when its visibility status changes. Between a\nhide and a show event, there is no need to waste CPU cycles preparing or displaying\ninformation to the user. A video application, for example, might simply stop generating\nnew frames.\n\nA widget that happens to be obscured by other windows on the screen is considered to be\nvisible. The same applies to iconified windows and windows that exist on another virtual\ndesktop (on platforms that support this concept). A widget receives spontaneous show and\nhide events when its mapping status is changed by the window system, e.g. a spontaneous\nhide event when the user minimizes the window, and a spontaneous show event when the\nwindow is restored again.\n\nYou almost never have to reimplement the setVisible() function. If you need to change\nsome settings before a widget is shown, use\n[showEvent](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#showEvent)() instead. If you\nneed to do some delayed initialization use the Polish event delivered to the\n[event](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#event)() function.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| bool | **isVisible**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| virtual void | **setVisible**(bool *visible*) |\n\n**See also** [show](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#show)(),\n[hide](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#hide)(),\n[isHidden](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#isHidden)(),\n[isVisibleTo](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#isVisibleTo)(),\n[isMinimized](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#minimized-prop)(),\n[showEvent](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#showEvent)(), and\n[hideEvent](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#hideEvent)().\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#visible"
    whatsThis: str
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### whatsThis : [QString](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qstring.html)\n\nThis property holds the widget's What's This help text.\n\nBy default, this property contains an empty string.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| QString | **whatsThis**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n| void | **setWhatsThis**(*const QString \\&*) |\n\n**See also** [QWhatsThis](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwhatsthis.html),\n[QWidget::toolTip](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#toolTip-prop), and\n[QWidget::statusTip](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#statusTip-prop).\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#whatsThis"
    width: int
    "Qt 5 reference:\n\n### width : const int\n\nThis property holds the width of the widget excluding any window frame\n\nSee the [Window\nGeometry](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/application-windows.html#window-geometry)\ndocumentation for an overview of geometry issues with windows.\n\n**Note:** Do not use this function to find the width of a screen on a [multiple screen\ndesktop](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qdesktopwidget.html). Read [this\nnote](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qdesktopwidget.html#screen-geometry) for\ndetails.\n\nBy default, this property contains a value that depends on the user's platform and\nscreen geometry.\n\n**Access functions:**\n\n| int | **width**() const |\n| --- | --- |\n\n**See also** [geometry](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#geometry-prop),\n[height](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#height-prop), and\n[size](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#size-prop).\n\nSource: https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qwidget.html#width"
    windowFilePath: str
